It's always a debate in balance. In nethack monster generation advances
with both the character and the dungeon depth. The real problem is that
charaters often advance much faster than thatn their capability to
handle the consequences of that advance. The lessons that the racket
teaches helps to smooth out that balance during the course of the game.
These lessons include:

1) A powerful pet can assist in the game. 

2) A character is not obligated to kill every monster they run across.
Sometimes it's better to simply avoid an encounter.

3) AC is a critical resource in the game. It's worth spending time
trying to find balance between AC, weight, and spellcasting/fighting
ability.

4) HPs are attainable without advancing level. These techniques are
useful in both the early and late stages of the game.

Now some folks (Xanthian comes to mind) overbalances the PR and
artificially underinflates XLs and overinflates AC even beyond the
initial reasonable goal of the PR (which is naked AC1). This is a
mistake because XLs also bring a laundry list of good things to the
table:

1) Hit points.
2) Power points.
3) Skill slots.
4) Intrinsics.
5) An absolute requirement to hit level 14 to complete the quest.

But unlike the PR list, the game balances those rewards with tougher
monster generation, which can be deadly in the early game.

Statistics on nh.org shows that soldier ants are the top cause of death
for players. A player with a -10AC, decent hit points, unburdened,
poison resistant and a level 9 pet will have very little problem with a
swarm of soldier ants. A PR player approaching Sokoban routinely will
fit all those criteria. However players who only have an awareness of
the benefits of XL may not have buffed the pet, or acquired the AC, or
may be burdended from excessive armor, or may not realize that running
away and fighting later is an option, may run into a different result.

Note that I'm not saying that running the racket is necessary for
success. What I am saying is that the more considered and cautious
approach that the racket necessitates better prepares players for what's
to come later.

I think my attitude is colored by the types of characters I play. Weak
spellcasting types that I play, such as the wizard, healer, and
archeologist that I've ascended, and the currently ascendable monk that
I have, can't just operate in tank mode in the early game. You get dead
and you get dead fast.

