No. The primary difference isn't the divine AC. The primary difference
is the deliberate slowing of experience gain. It's possible to do
exactly the same thing and never go into the mines to get the divine
protection. To a point it's not a bad strategy for any character because
it generates a strong pet, slows monster generation levels, and
emphesizes the dual strategy of lowering AC and running away. However,
it does it at the cost of not gaining all the good things that advancing
XLs gives you.

However, the divine AC prepares characters for midgame because it better
balances the AC requirements for midgame. Getting AC can impact 4 factors:

1) To hit.
2) damage.
3) spellcasting ability.
4) weight

The first two are the good aspects of AC. That's what all characters
want. The last two are the potential costs.

Armor is fine, but impacts all 4 factors. Magic AC is great because it
only has positive impacts (the first two) with none of the negative
impacts.  However, magic protection is difficult to attain in the early
game.  Characters generally do not know enchant armor and even if they
do, they have neither the mechanism to bless such scrolls or to generate
them reliably. Of course by mid game this is rarely a problem. But you
need to get there.

Divine AC has the same impacts as magic AC, but is attainable in the
early game because all it requires is a trip to Minetown and money. And
the costs are coupled to your XL. So doing it early and a lower XL is an
advantage.

My objective is to tip the balance of the game in my favor. Bumping XL
willy nilly tips the game in the favor of the RNG. I find that strategy
often more risky than trying to get my AC together first.

The real tipping points are the negative impacts of armor specifically
weight. Being burdened is a death sentence. High AC is a death sentence
too. Armor trades one for the other. Divine AC gives you the good impact
(low AC) without the bad impact (weight).

